  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**\n   The central idea is to develop an optimized strategy for the minimum overlap problem ininterval graphs by employing a multi-resolution optimization framework combined with advanced gradient-based search techniques. Unlike the parent solution, we will:\n   * Hypervoice: Use both low and high resolution samplings (n_s = 8, 256) initially before converging to high resolution grids for precision.\n   * Algo-centric optimization: Employ line search with gradient descent while maintaining analytical concepts from ergodic geometry to escape local minima early in the evolutionary history.\n   * Hypothesis: Fine-grained optimization finds better overlap reductions than coarse-only optimization; multi-grid techniques are likely more comprehensive.\n\n   We are aware that this requires increased computational resources, but given the task bounds, budget management will be critical.\n\n2. **Mathematicalspecification**\n   The solution space consists of piecewise-constant functions represented by samples over [0,2] with unit total mass. The optimization paradigm is:\n   Objective: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx over all k\n   Constraints: h(x) \u2208 [0,1], \u2211h(x) = 1 (integral condition effectively)\n\n   Let n_s be the sample count. The grid positions are x_i = i * (2/n_s) for i=0 to n_s-1.\n\n   The discrete correlation is used to approximate C_5.\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**\n   Implementation flow:\n   - Starting with n_s = 32, use uniform grid to initialize search\n   - Employ gradient descent with line search, ensuring:\n       \u2207E(h) is computed for E(h) = max_k correlation(h,1-h) scaled\n   - Check \u2207E(h) validity and direction, preventing oscillation\n   - Progress if descent direction shows positive improvement\n   - After n_s=32 iteration, increase resolution to n_s=256 and run gradient-based optimization\n   n_s management:\n      Lower during initial sampling fordata dimensionality reduction\n      Higher for precision and gradient satisfaction\n\n   Key helper functions:\n   - Gradient descent learning rate \u03b7 from line search (10-iterations)\n   - Check inf_p(integral) < 0.38\n   - Scale hyperparameters based on n_s\n\n4. **Search choices and variations**\n   Default config:\n   n_s_initial = 32\n   \u03b7_max = 0.01\n   grad_epsilon = 1e-4\n   Iterations per optimization: 200\n\n   More aggressive search would use:\n   - n_s_range = [8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256]\n   - Randomized initialization for each grid\n   But this is resource-heavy\n\n   Conservative fallbacks:\n   \u25b6 If two grid attempts > initial C_5, use finer grid\n   \u25b6 If descent fails early, switch to binary bucket search\n\n5. **Compute budget and robustness**\n   Stepwise n_s increases (soft budget check at 25% of time)\n   Operation-wise: Full correlation O(n_s^2) per descent\n   For n_s=256, each eval is about 50k operations -> in Python, few seconds\n   Total budget: Acceptable under 1300s\n\n   Recovery if numerical issues:\n   - If gradient not available, revert to finite-difference on subset\n   - If stuck at plateau, randomly perturb samples\n\n6. **Validation and return contract**\n   Solution must:\n   - Pass 0.0<=h(x)<=1 for all samples\n   - Satisfy sum(h) * (2/n_s) \u2248 1\n   - Compute plausible C_5 via correlation\n\n   include detailed comments in return value tags",
